+## Authority and roles
 
 The user supplies intent and grants authority. **Primary** is the interactive
-agent carrying the user's context, not the user. It resolves questions
-from owning sources, prior user decisions, and existing authority. It asks the
-user only when an unresolved answer materially changes product behavior,
-compatibility, ownership, scope, irreversible effects, or authority, or when
-explicit approval is still required. Obvious reversible choices within the
-assignment do not require escalation. Continue independent authorized work
-while waiting.
-
-Implementors and reviewers return decisions outside their assignments to Primary.
-An orchestrator owns automatic execution and stage progression within delegated
-authority, and routes unresolved product decisions to Primary. Returning a
-question to Primary does not itself require user intervention or grant new
-authority. Where tooling cannot deliver an escalation into the interactive
-session, user-mediated relay is a transport limitation, not the decision model.
-
-In older workflow records, “Human Primary” combines the user and interactive
-agent. Interpret those references through this distinction: user-owned approvals
-and proof remain with the user; context interpretation and delegated
-reconciliation belong to Primary. Provider account attribution is not an agent
-identity or additional authority. User policy exceptions follow
-[the KB policy](../README.md#user-authority-and-exceptions).
-
-## Normal objective
-
-```text
-The user authorizes one bounded objective
-  -> product parent operationally assigned to the native orchestrator
-  -> implementation stage
-      -> slice child 1 -> Implementor task(s) -> focused validation -> done
-      -> slice child 2 -> Implementor task(s) -> focused validation -> done
-  -> parent wake: orchestrator declares one coherent integrated surface
-  -> reusable integration-review child
-      -> Review Controller task(s) and fix loop -> clean -> done
-  -> parent wake: fresh acceptance-verdict child
-      -> Acceptance Reviewer verdict -> done
-  -> parent wake: accept, block, or resume the same review child
-  -> Primary authority and final product reconciliation
-```
-
-Primary carries the user-approved problem, architecture, product scope, slice
-boundaries, integration readiness, and completion criteria within delegated
-authority. The user retains final product and approval decisions. In a bounded
-automatic objective, the product parent is operationally assigned to the
-declared native orchestrator so terminal child stages wake the coordinator that
-can evaluate evidence and promote the next stage. Operational assignment does
-not transfer Primary's product authority, and the parent is never an
-Implementor task.
-
-The selected operational task resolves one execution target for the assigned
-role's environment. A project can expose one or many ordinary repository
-resources; their count does not activate `git-ws`. When no local workspace is
-bound for the target daemon, a capable runtime creates task-owned managed
-checkouts from the project repositories and the task explicitly checks out the
-repository or repositories named by its scope. The exact project URLs must also
-be declared and present in the operational workspace repository registry: the
-project resources select task context, while the registry supplies daemon
-checkout authorization and clone-cache synchronization. When the project
-instead binds an existing ordinary-Git or `git-ws` development workspace, the
-integration verifies exactly one matching path and daemon resource for that
-environment. Containerized Linux, native macOS, and future declared
-environments are peers rather than fallbacks. Missing project resources or
-workspace-registry coverage in managed-checkout mode, or a missing, duplicate,
-ambiguous, wrong-path, or wrong-daemon workspace binding in bound-workspace
-mode, fails closed. Changing a bound workspace remains a separate approved
-external-state mutation.
-
-When an approved slice uses a pull request as its durable code handoff, the
-handoff records the pull-request URL, source branch, and exact remote head
-commit. A later editing role verifies that head before acting and updates the
-same pull request only through a normal fast-forward push. A changed remote
-head makes earlier review or acceptance evidence stale; reconcile the new head
-instead of overwriting it. Force push, merge, close, or default-branch mutation
-requires separate exact authority and is never implied by ordinary pull-request
-delivery. Acceptance is bound to one exact live head, and any later head change
-requires fresh evaluation. Missing or null provider check data means no current
-passing evidence, not a green result.
-
-The pull request starts with the canonical title form
-`WIP: <issue-key>: <summary>`. The Implementor creates and verifies that title;
-the Review Controller preserves it throughout review, fixes, and validation;
-and the Acceptance Reviewer verifies it without changing provider state. The
-parent orchestrator is the workflow owner for the readiness prefix. It removes
-the leading `WIP:` only after the exact live head has passed all required
-implementation, review, acceptance, validation, platform, and user-owned runtime-proof gates. If the head changes or any gate reopens, it
-restores `WIP:` before staged work continues. An open or mergeable pull request,
-passing provider checks, an accepted verdict, or a completed issue does not by
-itself mean ready. Only reconciled removal of `WIP:` means ready for the user's merge decision; it does not authorize merge.
-
-Implementor owns delivery and focused validation of one coherent slice child.
-Review Controller starts the integrated review/fix cycle in fresh context,
-judges the actual combined repository surface, and retains that context while
-fixing, validating, and re-reviewing routine in-scope findings. A distinct
-Acceptance Reviewer judges the final surface read-only when practical.
-
-## Sequential objective lanes
-
-A project may opt into a sequential lane for approved top-level objectives.
-The lane is not another controller issue and does not alter the provider's task
-scheduler. It prevents competing objectives from becoming tasks until the
-current objective has finished or explicitly released the lane while awaiting an external gate.
-
-One project-owned marker identifies lane membership, and one durable
-human-visible ordering field defines the queue. Primary enrolls an
-approved parent by leaving it unassigned in `backlog`, attaching exactly one
-lane marker, and placing it at the intended order. The first visible eligible
-item executes first. New items append to the end unless Primary deliberately
-reprioritizes them. Queue members are top-level parents with an explicit
-operational project; children never carry lane markers. The marker remains on
-the parent while it occupies the lane and after terminal completion. A nonterminal
-parent waiting for user QA or an owning decision explicitly releases its marker
-under the rule below.
-
-The lane's orchestrator retains its current task through terminal parent
-reconciliation. After reading the parent back in an authorized terminal state,
-or after the nonterminal release procedure below, it obtains a stable read-only
-queue-head projection. The declared live marker
-must resolve exactly once; terminal lane history is not dispatch state. If a
-head exists, the orchestrator reads that complete parent and topology, verifies
-approval, lane, project, development-workspace binding, unassignment, and a
-stable task audit showing zero active parent and child tasks. It retrieves the
-exact records named for detailed reconciliation. Terminal task history and
-existing authoritative children are intake evidence, not blockers when no task
-is active; after activation the new parent run reconciles and adopts them
-through the ordinary adopt-before-create procedure. The orchestrator then
-projects the queue once more and requires the same head before moving it
-directly to `in_progress`
-without launching a task, reading it back, assigning itself exactly once, and
-verifying exactly one new task. An empty queue ends the handoff. A changing,
-occupied, malformed, or ambiguous projection permits no promotion.
-
-### Release a lane while awaiting an external gate
-
-Lane occupancy is not product completion. After implementation, integrated
-review and independent code/automated acceptance, user-owned GUI QA may remain
-open on a named child and exact candidate while the parent stays `in_review`
-and its PR retains `WIP:`. Do not require GUI capability for a code-only
-acceptance stage or call that verdict full product acceptance. Reuse an existing
-unstarted acceptance child for code acceptance when its GUI checks can be
-explicitly retained on a separate user-owned gate; never discard those checks.
-
-A named Primary decision may also release a lane once no independently executable
-work remains. Record the actual completed phase; this does not imply review,
-acceptance or feature completion. Do not use release for routine defects or work
-that the assigned agent can still perform.
-
-Before release, audit the complete parent/child task set: no child may be queued
-or running, and only the releasing orchestrator's current parent task may be
-active. Record the prior lane, exact candidate or retained work, completed gates,
-pending gate, owner, evidence needed, and resumption condition in the parent.
-Retain unmerged work and its identity; do not clean or overwrite a bound workspace.
-Persist `trackself_lane_release` metadata with that record, remove the parent's
-single queue label and verify read-back. The metadata is history, not a second
-queue selector. The existing queue projector then excludes the parked parent
-without weakening its checks for labeled parents. Follow ordinary queue handoff
-immediately; neither a terminal product status nor a user response is required.
-
-Parking releases capacity, not the approved objective's authority. On an external
-result, the coordinator owns completion: close when all gates pass, or re-enroll
-the same parent on its recorded original lane for remaining in-scope fixes or
-required evaluation. Default to the bottom of the queue; preserve an explicit
-user priority. A busy lane delays execution, not enrollment. Use ordinary lane
-admission, task read-back and a single launch trigger; never dispatch into an
-occupied lane or create a duplicate product objective. Clear the release marker
-when enrollment is verified. A routine fresh review is an executable next step,
-not a new user approval or waiver question.
-
-Explicit user QA acceptance resolves the named user-owned gate for the discussed
-candidate. Record it as user-reported acceptance, without inventing individual
-scenario observations. Missing optional screenshots, recordings or an agent-added
-checklist do not invalidate it. Preserve independent engineering acceptance:
-material changes require affected evaluation automatically, while unrelated
-administrative or documentation changes do not invalidate runtime QA. Name a
-concrete affected behavior before reopening that QA gate.
-
-Repair unambiguous stale status, links or result delivery using existing evidence;
-do not restart engineering evaluation for an administrative defect. Escalate only
-an actual unresolved scope/authority decision or technical prerequisite, with the
-precise owner and next action. End with verified completion, queued work, a running
-task, or a concrete blocker. An undelivered escalation must say so; ticket prose
-alone is not notification to Primary. No reliable asynchronous notification is
-assumed. See [ADR 0021 — objective completion](../decisions/0021-project-repositories-and-execution-targets.md#authorized-objective-completion-after-external-gates), which
-clarifies resumption ownership for [external gates](../decisions/0021-project-repositories-and-execution-targets.md#external-gates-and-execution-lane-occupancy).
-
-This ordering applies before task creation. Once a task exists, native provider
-priority and claim order govern it; changing board position cannot reorder an
-already queued task. Distinct lanes may progress concurrently only when each
-has its own declared orchestrator identity, runtime, daemon, and mutable state.
-When the provider binds an agent identity to one runtime, every specialist role
-used by that lane is likewise an environment-local identity whose behavior is
-projected from the shared role definition. A managed-repository lane has no
-matching project `local_directory` on its daemon and performs implementation in
-one task-owned checkout; a separate clean control workspace used to bootstrap
-instructions or workflow tooling is not an implementation surface. Adding a
-lane label does not itself create any of this runtime isolation. See
-[ADR 0021](../decisions/0021-project-repositories-and-execution-targets.md).
-
-## Human-readable objectives
-
-An operational issue is both an agent execution input and a human status
-surface. Every newly authored or materially rewritten human-facing operational
-issue objective starts with a self-contained executive summary following the
-[operational issue objective template](../templates/operational-issue-objective_template.md).
-A human must be able to understand the problem, intended outcome, importance,
-and current completion or blocking state without opening the linked documents.
-
-The objective then states its done criteria, authority boundaries, and owning
-references. When work is already underway, it also distinguishes completed
-work from remaining work; when blocked or staged, it names the next executable
-slice. Summaries explain linked truth without copying or redefining it.
-
-Tickets and PRs own assignments, review and delivery tracking under
-[artifact lifecycle](artifact-lifecycle.md). Maintain repository-owned intent,
-important decisions, limitations and resumption context even when a tracker is
-active. A bounded plan may summarize verified status to coordinate dependencies
-and readiness; reconcile its checkpoints and replace stale directions. Do not
-make essential context depend on a ticket provider remaining available.
-
-Operational history is evidence, not default startup context. A role normally
-reads its assigned objective, the current parent objective when present, owning
-artifacts, and actual repository state. It retrieves historical comments, task
-lists, and transcripts only for a referenced decision, ambiguity, recovery, or
-audit rather than ingesting the complete history on every run.
-
-Run or task completion does not close the objective. The operational issue
-status follows the stated engineering outcome and done criteria:
-
-- `in_progress` means executable implementation or review/fix work remains;
-- `in_review` means the current editing owner has completed its work and another
-  role owns the next evaluation in a manually coordinated issue;
-- `blocked` means no safe executable path exists without external resolution;
-- `done` means that issue's own deliverable is complete. For a product parent,
-  that includes its required review and acceptance gates; for an acceptance
-  child, it means a valid verdict was delivered even when that verdict rejects
-  or blocks the parent outcome.
-
-Severity, breadth, task completion, or the end of a bounded run does not make
-otherwise executable work blocked.
-
-The objective owner that launches an editing role owns the activation status
-transition. Before assignment, it moves the issue from `todo` or `backlog` to
-`in_progress` and reads that state back; only then does it assign the role and
-verify the resulting task. Assignment or a running task is not assumed to
-change issue status. If activation or assignment is ambiguous, the owner reads
-back both issue and task state before retrying or restoring the waiting status.
-The executing role verifies the assigned issue on entry but does not normally
-repair a missing startup transition. This keeps one owner responsible for the
-issue/task invariant: an editing task that is queued or running has an
-`in_progress` objective.
-
-## Implementation slices and task termination
-
-An engineering objective may require several coherent implementation slices.
-Each slice is small enough for one Implementor context to understand its
-premise, deliver one coherent outcome, run focused validation, leave an
-inspectable checkpoint, and determine whether the slice is complete. One slice
-may need several provider tasks after interruption or a premature checkpoint;
-the issue and role context remain the same while its outcome stays coherent.
-
-When operational tracking provides parent/child objectives, Primary keeps
-durable product authority. Under automatic staged execution, the declared
-native orchestrator is the product parent's operational assignee and creates
-one child for each materially distinct implementation slice. The child owns that slice's goal,
-surface, premise, focused validation, terminal condition, boundaries, owning
-references, and parent binding. It is not created per provider run: retries,
-interruptions, and ordinary same-role continuations remain on the same child.
-After a slice meets its own done criteria, its exact child contract may require
-the Implementor to reconcile the child's current-state section and criterion
-checkboxes against the final evidence, read the revised description back, post
-the final evidence, mark only that child `done`, and read it back. Closing the
-implementation-stage barrier wakes the parent orchestrator, which reconciles
-the slice outcomes and opens the next stage only when the approved parent still
-requires it.
-
-Automatic child creation is adopt-before-create and fail-closed. On every
-parent-orchestrator intake, and before creating, promoting, or assigning any
-child, the orchestrator reads the complete parent and obtains a complete
-structural projection of the existing child set, every child, and every child
-task history. When the project declares a stateless read-only context preparer,
-that tool performs the complete native retrieval and returns a bounded summary;
-raw descriptions, results, and histories do not enter model context by default.
-On a stage wake, the bounded projection includes the triggering child's
-current-state section so the orchestrator can detect a missing or contradictory
-`done` disposition without ingesting the complete description. Cancellation
-does not require satisfied deliverable criteria or a completed current-state
-section; its exact cancellation evidence governs reconciliation. The
-orchestrator then retrieves the exact triggering result and only the child or
-task records named for ambiguity, recovery, semantic matching, status/body
-reconciliation, or workspace-evidence verification. A malformed, truncated,
-unstable, incomplete, or over-limit projection permits no mutation; retry the
-read-only preparation or fall back to a complete manual audit before acting.
-
-A pre-created child that matches an approved parent outcome is authoritative
-and must be adopted; an orchestrator must not create a duplicate merely because
-it did not create that child. A compact projection never decides semantic
-matching or supersession. It exposes stages, titles, status, assignment,
-criterion counts, task disposition, role identity, and provider-session
-identity so the orchestrator can select the smallest exact detail expansion.
-The orchestrator reconciles duplicate, stale, superseded, or conflicting
-children before dispatch and stops for Primary resolution when
-supersession or ownership is ambiguous. Before assignment, it verifies that the
-lowest unfinished stage contains exactly its intended children, with no
-unintended non-terminal child holding the barrier, and that every later-stage
-child is unassigned and parked in `backlog`. Only then may it move the selected
-current-stage child to `in_progress`, read it back, and assign exactly one role.
-Human-authored parents should normally contain only the approved objective and
-proposed stage outline; if children are pre-created, the parent identifies them
-as authoritative and they begin unassigned in `backlog`.
-
-Slice completion normally requires focused tests and a clean, coherent,
-inspectable handoff, not a full independent review. Give a slice its own
-review/fix task only when it is independently risky, externally consumable, or
-difficult to reverse. That review remains on the slice issue unless a stronger
-context boundary is intentionally required.
-
-Completion authority must be explicit. By default, Primary or the
-operational objective owner reconciles the result and changes state. A child in
-a bounded automatic objective instead authorizes its executing specialist to
-reconcile that child's durable body and make only its `done` transition when
-the child's own deliverable is complete. Before the transition, the specialist
-evaluates every existing child-deliverable criterion against recorded evidence,
-updates those Markdown checkboxes and the current-state section without adding
-or redefining scope, states the exact remaining action or that none remains,
-persists the revised description, and reads it back. It then posts the final
-result, marks only that assigned child `done`, and reads back both description
-and status. For an implementation child, completion means slice criteria and
-focused validation passed with no separate slice-local review remaining. For
-the reusable integration-review child, it means the combined surface is clean.
-For an acceptance child, it means one valid `accepted`, `rejected`, or `blocked`
-verdict was delivered. Acceptance-child checkbox criteria therefore describe
-completion of the independent evaluation, evidence recording, and verdict
-delivery; product acceptance predicates are non-checkbox evaluation inputs and
-may fail without making a valid rejected or blocked verdict child incomplete.
-Issue-body reconciliation is operational recordkeeping and does not authorize a
-read-only Acceptance Reviewer to edit the reviewed surface. This narrow
-transition activates the native parent-stage notification; it does not
-authorize the role to change assignment, parent, siblings, later stages, or any
-other issue. The child-context run stops after read-back, and the resulting
-parent-context run alone interprets the evidence and advances the workflow.
-
-The automatic projection deliberately recognizes only the issue template's
-mechanical form: top-level `- [ ]` and `- [x]` lines inside the exact
-`## Done criteria` section, plus the exact top-level `## Current state`
-section. Agents and users must preserve that form for automated reconciliation;
-other Markdown list styles, nesting, and content outside those sections are
-ordinary prose. Do not put checkbox-like examples in the canonical top-level
-form inside `## Done criteria`.
-
-A `done` child with open child-deliverable criteria or a stale current-state
-section is incomplete orchestration even when its result comment appears
-credible. Cancellation does not require satisfied deliverable criteria; its
-separate cancellation and supersession evidence governs reconciliation. A
-missing triggering-child current-state section on a `done` child requires exact
-detail; a projected section that contradicts the terminal status or result
-blocks promotion even when every checkbox is closed. The parent orchestrator
-retrieves the exact evidence and does not promote the next stage until the body
-is reconciled. It may repair only this bookkeeping when the recorded result
-unambiguously proves every existing criterion of a `done` child; otherwise it
-returns reconciliation to the same specialist or escalates the ambiguity to
-Primary. It never checks a criterion merely to remove a structural
-warning.
-
-Once the completed slices form one coherent integrated surface, the woken
-orchestrator creates or promotes one reusable integration-review child under
-the product parent. Its objective binds the
-included slice issues, exact repository surface, integration criteria, required
-validation, and acceptance contract. Review Controller inspects the actual
-combined repositories rather than accepting slice reports as proof. Routine
-findings and fixes remain on this issue for the full cycle. When clean, the
-Review Controller completes that child and wakes the parent orchestrator.
-
-Every acceptance pass uses a fresh acceptance-verdict child assigned to a
-distinct read-only Acceptance Reviewer. The child is complete after it posts
-one valid verdict, reconciles its verdict-delivery criteria and current state,
-and marks itself `done`; rejection and blocked acceptance are verdicts for the
-parent, not blocked child execution. On routine rejection, the
-woken orchestrator reopens and reassigns the same Review Controller child,
-verifies provider-session continuity, and creates a new acceptance child only
-after the repaired surface is clean. Comments and finite specialist tasks are
-evidence; they are not continuation signals.
-
-An Implementor does not voluntarily end a successful task while safe,
-in-scope work toward its current slice remains and the runtime can
-continue. Useful partial progress, a reportable checkpoint, ordinary
-dependency or test-environment repair, validation debt, work breadth, or the
-end of an internal phase is not a terminal result. Progress evidence may be
-posted without ending the task.
-
-A normal Implementor task ends only when one of these is true:
-
-- the current slice's stated completion and validation condition is met;
-- no safe executable path remains without Primary or external resolution, and
-  the exact unresolved decision, evidence, access, or authority is reported;
-  or
-- the runtime is interrupted or reaches a real provider/resource boundary, in
-  which case the report names the preserved state and exact continuation point.
-
-If a task nevertheless ends after partial progress while the issue remains
-`in_progress`, orchestration treats it as a checkpoint, not a handoff to
-review. At `launch-on-command`, Primary explicitly resumes the same role after
-reconciling the actual workspace. At `automatic`, a project may define bounded
-same-role continuation, but only through its declared native task/session
-mechanics and without changing role, scope, or authority.
-
-## Automation levels
-
-Operational tracking must be active before workflow automation may be active.
-
-- `ticket-only`: the declared operational system may create/update tickets and
-  attach bounded evidence. People launch top-level role sessions manually.
-- `launch-on-command`: one explicit trigger authorizes one routine role run.
-- `automatic`: one approved bounded parent objective may progress through its
-  declared child stages, routine implementation, review/fix, fresh acceptance
-  passes, orchestrator reconciliation, validation, and local commit work
-  without a human trigger for every role run.
-
-The selected level is durable authorization for the declared project, ticket,
-repository scope, development target, and lower levels. Agents do not re-ask
-for routine actions already authorized by those boundaries.
-
-Workflow instructions and temporary task context affect agent behavior; they
-do not grant filesystem, network, credential, deployment, or provider-mutation
-capabilities. Those remain controlled by the execution environment's
-permission configuration and isolation boundary. When an automated runtime
-injects temporary instructions into a development workspace also used by a
-human-started session, it must project them into runtime-private state rather
-than changing the human-visible authoritative or root instruction file.
-
-That authority does not include another slice, deployment, remote host
-mutation, host/system package installation or removal, execution-environment
-image or service administration, privileged/system changes, destructive
-workspace rewriting, or changing the automation level. A push also retains its
-normal explicit approval boundary unless the project declares the selected
-managed-repository lane to be a staged pull-request delivery lane as described
-below.
-
-### Managed-repository delivery baton
-
-Enrollment of a user-approved parent in a project-declared staged
-pull-request lane authorizes the minimum remote Git transport needed for that
-parent's automatic workflow: create a task feature branch, commit the complete
-validated slice, push it normally without rewriting remote history, and create
-or update one issue-linked pull request. The Implementor records the pull
-request URL, source branch, and exact remote head before its child can complete.
-It creates the pull request with the canonical `WIP:` title prefix and verifies
-the live title. The prefix remains present throughout implementation,
-integration review, acceptance, and any required runtime proof.
-The Review Controller checks out that exact remote head in its own task-managed
-checkout, commits routine in-scope fixes, updates the same pull request only by
-normal fast-forward push, preserves `WIP:`, and records the new remote head.
-Each Acceptance Reviewer evaluates the exact live pull-request head and title
-read-only and rejects stale handoff evidence.
-
-The parent orchestrator narrowly owns the readiness-prefix transition. It
-removes the leading `WIP:` only when every required gate for the exact live head
-has passed, including user-owned runtime proof when the objective
-requires it, then reads the title and head back. That transition marks the pull
-request ready for the user's merge decision and grants no merge authority.
-If a head changes or a gate reopens after removal, the orchestrator restores
-`WIP:` before continuing. Provider `open`, `mergeable`, check, verdict, and
-issue states never substitute for this contract.
-
-This transport authority is supplied by deliberate lane selection and does not
-need to be repeated in every child. It never authorizes force push, merge,
-pull-request closure, default-branch mutation, tagging, release, publication of
-artifacts, or deployment. A parent that intentionally forbids remote feature-
-branch or pull-request publication is incompatible with a managed-repository
-staged pull-request lane and must use a suitable bound workspace or remain
-manually driven.
-
-Task-owned managed checkouts are isolation boundaries, not an informal source
-exchange. A later role must not read, copy, or reconstruct implementation from
-a sibling task checkout during normal progression. Retained task directories
-may support orchestrator evidence reconciliation or an explicitly authorized
-recovery, but ordinary cross-role continuity is the verified Forgejo branch,
-pull request, exact remote commit, and operational task record. Missing
-credentials, failed publication, a changed remote head, or an unavailable PR is
-a real handoff failure; it is not permission to substitute shared on-disk
-state.
-
-Routine development setup inside the assigned development workspace is not
-package or system installation for this purpose. Unless project or repository
-instructions narrow it, an authorized implementation role may hydrate declared
-dependencies into repository-local ignored environments, use project caches,
-run formatters and generators, create build/test artifacts, start development
-processes, and create, migrate, reset, or remove disposable test databases. It
-may make ordinary ticket-scoped file and Git working-tree changes without
-requesting separate permission for each command.
-
-Automatic progression requires a native durable continuation path. When the
-operational system wakes a parent assignee only after a child stage closes, the
-declared native orchestrator is the parent's operational assignee and each
-specialist child receives exact child-only completion authority. A comment,
-task completion, or reassignment does not substitute for that wake. The parent
-orchestrator reads back the terminal child and its task evidence before it
-promotes another stage. If the operational system cannot provide this boundary,
-the project must remain manually driven rather than claiming automatic
-progression.
-
-A separately activated liveness watchdog may recover a missed native wake. It
-detects an `in_progress` orchestrator-owned parent with no active orchestrator
-or child task and either a non-terminal stage or an undispatched next child,
-then wakes the orchestrator to run the same intake reconciliation. It does not
-create, cancel, promote, assign, or change issue status itself. Repository
-definition of this contract does not authorize installing or scheduling the
-watchdog; that operational activation retains its project-specific approval
-boundary.
+agent carrying the user's context. It resolves routine questions from owning
+sources and prior decisions, and asks the user when an unresolved answer
+materially changes product behavior, compatibility, ownership, scope,
+irreversible effects, or authority, or when explicit approval is required.
+Continue independent authorized work while an answer is pending.
+
+Implementors and reviewers return decisions outside their assignments to
+Primary. A coordinator may automate only actions delegated by the user or
+active project policy. Coordination mechanics do not transfer product,
+architecture, review, repository, or approval authority. Provider account
+attribution is not an agent identity or additional authority. Scoped user
+policy exceptions follow [the KB policy](../README.md#user-authority-and-exceptions).
+Execution permissions remain enforced by the runtime and repository rules.
+
+The selected policy version is pinned to each ticket across runs and retries.
+New defaults apply to new tickets; existing work changes policy only through
+explicit migration or a scoped override. Record the base policy identity and
+any user or delegated supervisor exception with its reason, scope, and
+consequences. Neither agent definitions nor generated instructions may silently
+reinterpret existing tickets or impose incompatible hidden workflow rules.
+
+## Objectives and durable records
+
+An issue should state the observable outcome, scope and relevant boundaries,
+completion evidence, and links to owning truth. Make it understandable to a
+person without requiring them to read a provider transcript. For work already
+underway, add only the current state or next owner needed to resume it. Keep
+repository-owned intent, decisions, limitations, and resumption context in the
+owning artifacts; an operational ticket is not their sole durable copy.
+
+Keep related implementation and review/fix work on its owning objective while
+its outcome remains coherent. A provider run ending, interruption, model change,
+role handoff, review pass, or status transition does not by itself require a new
+issue or subtask. A subtask is useful when it represents an independently
+trackable outcome, real parallel work, a distinct owner or environment, or a
+dependency that benefits from separate coordination. Decomposition is a choice
+about coordination, not a required workflow shape. When a parent/child
+relationship exists, completion of a child is evidence for the parent; it does
+not itself establish acceptance of the parent outcome.
+
+Tickets carry ownership, current status, scope, blockers, acceptance state, and
+links to relevant work. Code review systems carry the technical findings,
+fixes, validation, and independent verdict for the exact code candidate. Link
+these records rather than copying full technical reports into every issue.
+
+For a pull-request handoff, record the PR URL, source branch, and exact head
+commit. A later editing or reviewing role checks that identity before acting.
+A changed head invalidates any evaluation or acceptance affected by the change
+and requires reconciliation against the new candidate. One ticket may coordinate
+several PRs; acceptance identifies each relevant commit. Normal delivery does not imply
+force-push, merge, close, release, or deployment authority.
+
+Operational history is evidence, not default startup context. Read the assigned
+objective, applicable owning artifacts, and actual repository state first.
+Retrieve older comments, task lists, or transcripts only to resolve a referenced
+decision, ambiguity, recovery, or audit. Evidence stored outside the repository
+should be bounded, purpose-limited, and redacted; do not export secrets or
+unnecessary raw content.
+
+## Status, ownership, and completion
+
+The selected operational system and active project policy define the exact
+status vocabulary, assignment behavior, trigger paths, and transitions. They
+must make clear who owns the next action. A status label alone is not proof that
+a task launched, a handoff completed, or an outcome was accepted.
+
+Use these outcome distinctions when mapping a system's states:
+
+- Executable implementation, review, or in-scope correction remains active.
+- Independent review or human acceptance is pending only when a named owner
+  and exact candidate are clear. An assigned reviewer or fix owner may still
+  have executable work even if the system uses a review-related status.
+- Work is blocked only when no safe path remains without missing evidence,
+  access, authority, an external prerequisite, or an unresolved decision.
+- An issue is complete only when its own stated outcome has been accepted or
+  completed under the active policy. A task ending, valid rejection, or child
+  completion does not silently complete a different product outcome.
+
+Before dispatch, select the native trigger that will launch the intended role
+and verify the resulting task or handoff. Do not combine two launch triggers.
+Treat an ambiguous mutation response as unknown state: read back the issue,
+comment, and task state before retrying, and do not repeat work that may already
+have started. The integration owns exact recovery behavior and must prevent
+stale or duplicate dispatch.
+
+## Implementation and independent review
+
+Implementation roles own their assigned outcome and proportional focused
+validation. Before a resumed writer edits, it reconciles intervening repository
+changes, decisions, scoped exceptions, and candidate identity with the current
+ticket and owning artifacts. Retained conversation is not proof that earlier
+assumptions remain current. Routine in-scope review findings return to an
+editing context that retains useful work and repository understanding across
+fixes, validation, and re-review. If continuity is lost, reconstruct from
+owning artifacts, the exact candidate, and execution evidence; do not claim a
+replacement context continued the prior conversation.
+
+When the active policy requires independent final review, use a distinct,
+fresh, read-only reviewer context. Give it the complete exact candidate and
+relevant validation evidence without steering it toward an expected verdict.
+The reviewer reports its own findings and verdict. It does not edit the
+reviewed surface or accept delegated changes on exit status alone. In-scope
+findings return to the retained editing context; after affected code changes,
+obtain a new evaluation of the changed candidate. A new request outside the
+approved scope is a scope decision, not a defect to fold silently into the
+existing objective.
+
+Review independence is a property of the evaluation, not of issue count. A
+separate review ticket may be useful for ownership or coordination, but a new
+issue, task ID, or run ID alone does not prove a fresh context or an independent
+verdict.
+
+The active policy defines review depth and any justified scoped exceptions.
+Classifying a ticket as trivial does not alone waive required independent
+review. A review/fix agent cannot be the sole final judge of its own corrections.
+For work without a PR, retain equivalent exact-result review evidence in the
+appropriate owning record without manufacturing a code handoff.
+
+## Optional decomposition and staged workflows
+
+Projects may explicitly select a staged parent/child workflow when their
+operational system and task shape benefit from one. Its owning integration must
+declare the lifecycle, child authority, exact status transitions, dispatch and
+wake behavior, reconciliation, and recovery in its own versioned instructions.
+It must not be inferred from an automation level, a generic request for review,
+or the presence of subtasks. Projects that do not declare this pattern use the
+selected workflow on the owning issue and decompose only where coordination
+benefits from it.
+
+Do not create one child per role, provider run, review pass, or verdict as a
+general requirement. A project that deliberately adopts such a structure owns
+its extra lifecycle and must preserve the authority and review boundaries above.
 
